======================================================================
MACH Device Features
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Value Proposition
       MACH is a significant milestone in the evolution of PLD type
       architectures -- it provides high logic density with no compromise in
       performance. The device features and flexibility provided are
       "balanced" and optimized for efficient software allocation.
       
       Further, the advanced EE-CMOS technology provides a very
       manufacturable, easily testable "canvas" for expressing new system
       architectures and getting designs quickly and economically to market.
       MACH will be an important vehicle for developing the customized logic
       for systems of the '90's.

MACH110 -- Core Features:
       
         - Multiple PAL-block structure -- Programmable AND/OR logic
         - Architecture rich in macros -- Combinatorial, D and T-type
              FF Equations
         - Variable Product Term Allocation - up to 12-in OR
         - Global signal routing resource for inputs, logic feedbacks
         - Selection of two clock sources at each register
         - Banks of three-state enables, FF Set and Reset
         - Independent feedback from internal buried node and pin sources
         - Push-button Fitting Software, fully supporting all resources



Additional MACH 210 features
         - Increased PT capability (up to 16-in OR)
         - Additional signal routing resources
         - Additional logic equation type -- Transparent Latch
         - Input Storage modes -- Register and latch
         - More banks - independent set and reset

Value to User
       The multiple PAL-block structure is popular and easily  learned. Its
       AND/OR logic implementation is efficient and fast when its size is
       22-inputs and 70 product terms. The variable PT allocation resource
       makes it possible to develop large logic functions on pins of your
       choice, without the overhead (die size and speed) of dedicated fixed
       OR terms.


